Appendix A: DSA Cheat Sheet - Patterns and Time Complexities
This is your quick-reference for every Data Structures and Algorithms pattern you will encounter in internship 
interviews. Study this alongside Striver's A2Z sheet. For every problem you solve, map it to one of these 
patterns.
A.1 Sliding Window Pattern
Use when: Finding max/min/sum of a subarray of fixed or variable size. Typical problems: Maximum sum 
subarray of size K, Longest substring without repeating characters, Minimum window substring.
// Template: Fixed window
int left = 0, windowSum = 0, maxSum = 0;
for (int right = 0; right < nums.size(); right++) {
 windowSum += nums[right];
 if (right - left + 1 == k) {
 maxSum = max(maxSum, windowSum);
 windowSum -= nums[left++];
 }
}
// Template: Variable window
int left = 0; unordered_map<char, int> freq;
int maxLen = 0;
for (int right = 0; right < s.size(); right++) {
 freq[s[right]]++;
 while (freq.size() > k) { // condition to shrink
 freq[s[left]]--;
 if (freq[s[left]] == 0) freq.erase(s[left]);
 left++;
 }
 maxLen = max(maxLen, right - left + 1);
}
A.2 Two Pointer Pattern
Use when: Sorted array, finding pairs that sum to target, removing duplicates, palindrome check. Time: O(n). 
Space: O(1).
// Template: Two sum in sorted array
int left = 0, right = nums.size() - 1;
while (left < right) {
 int sum = nums[left] + nums[right];
 if (sum == target) return {left, right};
 else if (sum < target) left++;
 else right--;
}

